{"name":"Scalloped Oak","hex":"#f2d1a0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"scalloped","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of scallop"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having an edge or border marked with semicircles.","Cooked gratin; baked in a typical type of sauce, usually with cheese, as a casserole, e.g. scalloped potatoes; scalloped corn"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/scalloped"},{"term":"oak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Noakhali."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deciduous tree with distinctive deeply lobed leaves, acorns, and notably strong wood, typically of England and northeastern North America, included in genus Quercus.","The wood of the oak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Scalloped oak","blocks":[{"text":"The scalloped oak is a moth of the family Geometridae. The species was first described by Carl Linnaeus in his 1758 10th edition of Systema Naturae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Crocallis_elinguaria_%28Scalloped_oak%29%2C_Nijmegen%2C_the_Netherlands.jpg/330px-Crocallis_elinguaria_%28Scalloped_oak%29%2C_Nijmegen%2C_the_Netherlands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scalloped_oak","wikidata":"Q1602800"},{"title":"Scallop","blocks":[{"text":"Scallop is a common name that encompasses various species of marine bivalve molluscs in the taxonomic family Pectinidae, the scallops. However, this common name is also sometimes applied to species in other closely related families within the superfamily Pectinoidea, which also includes the thorny oysters."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Argopecten_irradians.jpg/330px-Argopecten_irradians.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scallop","wikidata":"Q1311395"},{"title":"Oak","blocks":[{"text":"An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 475 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak","wikidata":"Q12004"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Geometridae_-_Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg/500px-Geometridae_-_Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Geometridae_-_Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg/500px-Geometridae_-_Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeometridae_-_Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg/500px-Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg/1280px-Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg/500px-Crocallis_elinguaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrocallis_elinguaria.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Crocallis_elinguaria_larva.jpg/500px-Crocallis_elinguaria_larva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Crocallis_elinguaria_larva.jpg/1280px-Crocallis_elinguaria_larva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Crocallis_elinguaria_larva.jpg/500px-Crocallis_elinguaria_larva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Larva","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrocallis_elinguaria_larva.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Opened_scallop_shell.jpg/500px-Opened_scallop_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Opened_scallop_shell.jpg/500px-Opened_scallop_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A live opened scallop showing the internal anatomy: The pale orange circular part is the adductor muscle; the darker orange curved part is the \"coral\", a culinary term for the ovary or roe.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOpened_scallop_shell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Fast_and_slow_adductor_muscles_of_the_Yesso_scallop%2C_Patinopecten_yessoensis.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Fast_and_slow_adductor_muscles_of_the_Yesso_scallop%2C_Patinopecten_yessoensis.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Adductor muscles of a scallop. a: photo in a live scallop. b: after removal of organs and tissues.","link":"https://en.wikiped